   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "The pieces of a conversation",
     "texto": [
      "A conversation has small pieces that help you tell, listen and change the subject:\n\n- **Opening a story**: **Oye, tengo que contarte una cosa.** **¿Sabes qué ha pasado?** The other person answers **¿Qué ha pasado?** or **Dime**.\n- **Showing you listen**: **¿En serio?** **¿De verdad?** **¡Vaya!** **Ya.** **¡Qué bien!**\n- **Moving the story on**: **de repente** (something unexpected), **y entonces**, **al final** (the outcome).\n- **Checking**: **¿me entiendes?** On the phone: **¿me oyes?**, **te oigo fatal**, **se ha cortado**.\n- **Interrupting and giving the turn**: **Perdona, pero…**, **Un momento…**; **Sigue, sigue**, **Déjame terminar, por favor**.\n- **Changing the subject**: **por cierto**, **otra cosa**, **en cuanto a**.\n- **Closing**: **en resumen**, **para terminar**; in an informal chat, **bueno, pues nada**, and then **vale, nos vemos**.\n\nTone changes the meaning: **déjame terminar** can sound firm."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Telling recent news",
     "texto": [
      "To tell something recent, use the **present perfect**: **haber** + participle. *¿Qué ha pasado?* *El jefe ha dicho que se va.*\n\nThe story words put events in order:\n\n- **de repente** + a surprising event;\n- **y entonces** + what follows;\n- **al final** + the result.\n\nTo report what someone said, use **ha dicho que** + the verb. After **ha dicho** the next verb does not change tense: *ha dicho que viene*, *ha dicho que se va*."
     ],
